k if I had this  my time trying to study and rista Diann  and relearn and memorize you know  grammar rules or tables or answer  questions or do any of this sort of you  know study type work I wouldn't be able  to put much time in it I would I would  just stop doing it so in that sense  perhaps I would be burned out but when I  look at my check now for example you  know I've I happen to go over to  Vancouver Island and in Victoria there's  a store there it's an online store it's  called check ebooks.com I think it is  it's it's a wonderful place to buy all  kinds of stuff and I've bought things  from them via the internet but I thought  I'm in Victoria I may as well go and see  and see what's there and because it's  nothing like actually looking at books  and so I bought a bunch of CDs and DVDs  and books so I bought a book here called  you know this one here okay Molly yeh  yeh eeny Cheska's Lewinsky short history  of Czechoslovakia room 1867 or 1939  now as I read I'm starting into it  there's words that I don't know but it's  very very interesting here you have the  austro-hungarian Empire you have the  german-speaking group who are basically  the if you want the colonial power much  like England was in Ireland sort of  thing they you know had expanded  eastward and dominated the Austrian  Empire eventually the austro-hungarian  Empire so they had the privileges that  German language was given you know  preeminence and so on and so on and  gradually the Czechs through the 19th  century start to achieve some  recognition of themselves as an  independent state and then you have the  First World War and now the you know the  shoe is on the other foot the Czechs  want to set up their own state what  rights are the german-speaking minority  the ones who've been lording it over the  Czechs and denying them their language  rights and trying to impose German sort  of as part of the whole german-speaking  Austral or Austria  you know and so you have these this  whole issue of how that evolves and of  course these are all things that I  didn't really focus on and it's true  that the german-speaking minority in  Czechoslovakia got the short end of the  stick and that might have contributed to  events leading up to the Second World  War but then again they had basically  you know denied or tried to suppress the  Czechs more or less for quite a long  time so it's very interesting I'm also  listening to other things that are in  interest I'm listening to daily news  discussions in check so I'm kind of  familiar with issues like corruption and  and and so forth that are hot you know  in the in the Czech Republic so that's  all interesting so everything I'm really  and I'm following the international news  I'm following the economic crisis in  Europe via the Czech media so it's  interesting when I get tired of doing  that I did find a very good book again  over there called Chesky croaked Zac  crook M which is you know simpler  material where I can get more of the  sort of everyday language which I heard  but I need to listen to it more and more  in order to be able to use it and it has  some you know the odd grammar  explanation which I don't mind looking  at especially as I become more and more  curious about certain things and so as  long as you vary it and do things that  are interesting I don't see how you can  get you know bird out when I was  studying Chinese I mean you know there's  so much there history literature modern  history ancient history I mean how do  you get burned out I think the key thing  is but you you you have a goal beyond  just learning the language if your goal  is just to learn the language just to  nail down these declension tables or  whatever it might be yeah I can see  where you get burned out because after a  while those things won't penetrate  whereas if you allow yourself to just  learn things and learn about things by  interacting with the language and  reading and listening in if you have an  opportunity like I also borrowed some or  bought some DVDs some movies but I can't  understand them yet without the  subtitles  I can't understand some of the dialogue  but basically can't understand the  dialogue so I put them aside that's a  bit of a goal now maybe two months from  now I'll be able to understand more of  the movies so if you keep it varied like  that as I say with Chinese there were so  many different things that I could do  with the language I I didn't get burned  out but I guess at the core of it all  you have to be interested in so you have  to do things that are interesting in  order to maintain your interest so I  don't to me burnout is not a problem and  if you want to spend less time at it you  spend less time I'm not under any  obligation to do anything with check I'm  doing it you know out of interest but I  guess if someone is at university and  they have to you know pass some exams  and they're sitting there trying to jam  or Ram certain declension tables or  rules into their heads yeah they can get  burned out  I still think those people would be  better off if they spent their time  doing interesting things with the  language and eventually these things  that are trying to ram into their brain  will fall into place so there you have  it burn out for me it's not an issue as  long as you're doing things that are  interesting and when you are less  interested or not interested you stop  doing them no burn out
 

